use Libraries.Web.Page.QuorumPage

/*
    Lab 6.3: Inheritance Practice.
    Converted from the old Lesson page by plan 13's generator: the same
    words, now without Tailwind.
*/
class Lab6_3 is QuorumPage
    // A double quote, for code and words that hold one.
    text dq = ""

    on create
        SetLocation("lessons/chapter6/lab6_3.html")
        // The name lists and links use, and the series and track it belongs
        // to: its section and picture.
        SetShortTitle("Lab 6.3")
        // For search engines and link previews, and what lists of pages say.
        SetSummary("another practice session with inheritance")
    end

    action Setup
        dq = dq:GetDoubleQuote()
        SetWebPageTitle("Lab 6.3: Inheritance Practice")
        Title("Lab 6.3: Inheritance Practice")
        Paragraph("A practice session with inheritance")
        Objectives()
        Overview()
        Task1GettingStarted()
        Task2CreatingYourParentClass()
        Task3InheritingYourPetClass()
        Task4UsingInheritedActions()
        SampleOutput()
    end

    private action Objectives
        Section("Objectives")
        Paragraph("The goal of this lab is to understand the following concepts:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem("How to use inheritance across multiple classes")
        ListItem("How to create and use a class hierarchical system")
        ListItem("How to use polymorphism")
    end

    private action Overview
        Section("Overview")
        Paragraph("In this lab, you will create a program to ask the user about their pets. It will collect some information from then, and then tell them about their pet based on the info provided. Because many pets have some of the same attributes (they eat, play, sleep, etc.), you can use inheritance to create multiple subclasses of different pets.")
    end

    private action Task1GettingStarted
        Section("Task 1: Getting Started")
        Paragraph("Start Sodbeans. Create a new “Quorum Application” project, and name it Lab6_3. In the Main.quorum file, it should contain a Main class and Main action.")
        Paragraph("You will create four additional classes in this project. In the New File dialog, create a new file by selecting “Quorum” and “Quorum Class” in the Categories and File Types windows, respectively. Then, name the new file Pet in the New Quorum Class dialog. Repeat the above steps to create Classes named Cat, Dog, and Lizard.")
    end

    private action Task2CreatingYourParentClass
        Section("Task 2: Creating Your Parent Class")
        Paragraph("In your Pet class, create variables to store the following data:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem("a number type to store the weight")
        ListItem("a text type to store food")
        ListItem("a text type to store gender")
        ListItem("an integer type to store number of hours of sleep")
        ListItem("an integer type to store number of hours of playtime")
        ListItem("an integer type to store number of toys")
        Paragraph("Remember to follow good naming convention practices when creating your variables.")
        Paragraph("Next, create access modifiers and accessor actions for weight, sleep, playtime, and toys. Create: action PetNoise")
        Paragraph("When called, PetNoise should say "):DoubleQuote():Text("generic pet noise."):DoubleQuote():Text(" Later, you will use polymorphism to change this default message into something else for each of your pet classes. Polymorphism is the idea that some variables, operations, or objects behave differently in different contexts. For example, in Sodbeans, the + (plus) operator can be used in different ways:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem("4 + 5 //integer addition")
        ListItem("5.1 + 5.0 //floating point addition (number addition)")
        ListItem("string1 + "):DoubleQuote():Text("hello!"):DoubleQuote():Text(" //string concatenation")
        Paragraph("In the above example, the operator + is being used in several different ways, depending on the context in which it is called.")
    end

    private action Task3InheritingYourPetClass
        Section("Task 3: Inheriting Your Pet Class")
        Paragraph("Now that your parent class has the needed actions, you will implement classes Cat, Dog, and Lizard. Using inheritance, write actions for each of the before mentioned classes that will prompt the user to input a value that will be used for each of the setter actions you made in class Pet (weight in pounds, sleep in hours, playtime in hours, and number of toys). Also, implement the PetNoise action in such a way that it overrides the default message with a noise that each of the respective animals would make (woof, meow, etc.). Recall that you had two variables of type text, one for food, and one for gender in the parent class. For each of the child classes, create actions that will return the text entered by the user for these two variables. An example of such an action might look like this:")
        CodeExample("class Cat is Pet
    action AskWeight()
        number pounds = cast(number, input(" + dq + "How heavy is your cat?" + dq + ")) //casting to type number is done in one go with an input statement.
        parent:Pet:SetWeight(pounds) //uses the keyword parent to access actions in the Pet class.
    end
end", "")
        Paragraph("First, notice that the variable pounds has to be cast to type number. In the above example, this is being done on the same line as the input statement, but it doesn't have to be. Second, notice that the action SetWeight, which was declared in class Pet, is being accessed by the keyword parent. The parent keyword allows us to reference a class directly.")
    end

    private action Task4UsingInheritedActions
        Section("Task 4: Using Inherited Actions")
        Paragraph("In Main, instantiate objects of your Cat, Dog, and Lizard classes. Next, ask the user what kind of pet they have, 1 for cat, 2 for dog, or 3 for lizard. You will now need to create control structures that perform like the following:")
        Paragraph("if cat:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem("get values for weight, sleep, play, and toys")
        ListItem("get food type")
        ListItem("get gender")
        ListItem("tell user in complete sentences about their pet")
        Paragraph("if dog:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem("do the same as above")
        Paragraph("if lizard:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem("do the same as above")
        Paragraph("If the user inputs anything but 1, 2, or 3, prompt them again to enter 1 for cat, 2 for dog, or 3 for lizard. Also, add control structures so that if the pet is male, it is referred to as "):DoubleQuote():Text("he"):DoubleQuote():Text(", and if it is female, it is referred to as "):DoubleQuote():Text("she."):DoubleQuote():Text(" Regardless of which derived class is called, make sure to call the derived action PetNoise. If polymorphism is used correctly, the user should hear what noise their pet makes, instead of the default message.")
    end

    private action SampleOutput
        Section("Sample Output")
        Paragraph("Here is what the program may look like if the user says they have a cat:")
        CodeExample("What kind of pet do you have? Enter 1 for Cat, 2 for Dog, or 3 for Lizard.
1
Meow!
How heavy is your cat?
10
What kind of food does your cat eat?
kitten chow
Is your cat a boy or girl? Enter 1 for male, 2 for female.
1
How many hours a day does your cat sleep?
16
How many hours a day do you play with your cat?
2
How many toys does your cat have?
5
Your cat weighs 10 pounds and eats kitten chow
Your cat is a boy and sleeps 16 hours a day. He plays 2 hours a day and has 5 toys to play with.", "")
        Paragraph("When you are done, debug and fix any errors, then show your code to your instructor.")
    end
end
